.TH SHARKY 1 "2026-10-28" "sharky 1.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sharky \- Unix-first interactive shell written in Ruby
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sharky
[\fB\-\-no-config\fR] [\fB\-i\fR]
.br
.B sharky
\fB\-c\fR \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARG\fR...]
.br
.B sharky
\fISCRIPT\fR [\fIARG\fR...]
.br
.B sharky
\fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.br
.B sharky
\fB\-\-json\fR \fB\-c\fR \fICOMMAND\fR
.br
.B sharky
\fBstate\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-env\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sharky
is an interactive shell that owns its parser, expansion engine, executor,
editor, prompt, completion and job control. Commands run as real Unix
processes. It is written in Ruby 3.4 using only the standard library and
default gems.
.PP
A native Bash-like subset is supported in \fB\-c\fR, in scripts ending in
\fB.sh\fR, through a \fB#!/usr/bin/env sharky\fR shebang, and in the interactive
editor. Login-shell support is deliberately post-v1.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c \fICOMMAND\fR
Run one shell command string and exit with its status.
.TP
.B \-i
Start interactively (requires a TTY).
.TP
.B \-\-no-config
Do not load the configuration directory.
.TP
.B \-\-json
With \fB\-c\fR, emit one JSON object on stdout containing status, stdout,
stderr, duration, cwd and environment delta.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH LANGUAGE SUBSET
Supported: quoting, variables, positional parameters, parameter expansion
(defaults, assignment, alternate, length, trim, substitution), arithmetic
expansion and commands, brace expansion, indexed arrays, globs, command
substitution, lists, pipes, redirects, here-documents, here-strings,
subshells, background jobs, \fBif/elif/else\fR, \fBfor\fR, \fBwhile\fR,
\fBuntil\fR, \fBcase\fR, functions, groups, \fB!\fR, \fB[[ ]]\fR, \fBtrap\fR
(EXIT action executes), \fBbreak\fR, \fBcontinue\fR and \fBreturn\fR.
.PP
Builtins include \fBcd\fR, \fBpwd\fR, \fBexport\fR, \fBsource\fR, \fB.\fR,
\fBset\fR, \fBread\fR, \fBshift\fR, \fBjobs\fR, \fBfg\fR, \fBbg\fR, \fBwait\fR,
\fBkill\fR, \fBalias\fR, \fBunalias\fR, \fBtype\fR, \fBhistory\fR, \fBz\fR and
\fBexit\fR. Shell options include \fBset -e\fR (errexit), \fBset -u\fR
(nounset), \fBset -x\fR (xtrace) and \fBset -o pipefail\fR.
.PP
Not supported in v1: full Bash/POSIX, \fBcoproc\fR, process substitution,
extended globs, associative arrays, array slices, custom \fBIFS\fR,
programmable completion, full \fB[[ =~ ]]\fR parity and login-shell installation.
.SH SCRIPTS
A file named \fB*.sh\fR, or a file whose first line is
\fB#!/usr/bin/env sharky\fR, runs through the native parser. The script path is
\fB$0\fR; arguments become \fB$1\fR, \fB$2\fR, ...; \fB$#\fR is the count.
\fBsource FILE [ARG...]\fR (or \fB. FILE\fR) runs a file in the current shell.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration directory is \fB$SHARKY_CONFIG\fR or
\fB${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/sharky\fR. It contains \fBsharky.rb\fR,
\fBthemes/*.toml\fR and \fBfunctions/*.rb\fR. \fBinit.rb\fR is accepted as a
legacy fallback.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHARKY_CONFIG
Override the configuration directory.
.TP
.B SHARKY_DEBUG
Set to \fB1\fR for Ruby script backtraces.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sharky/sharky.rb
User configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sharky/history
Command history.
.SH JSON
\fBsharky --json -c CMD\fR writes exactly one object:
.nf
{"schema_version":1,"status":0,"stdout":"","stderr":"",
 "duration_ms":1,"cwd":"/tmp","env_delta":{}}
.fi
\fBsharky state --json\fR writes schema_version, cwd, last_status and pid.
Pass \fB--env\fR to include the full environment explicitly; it is omitted by
default so secrets do not leak into logs.
.SH JOBS
Background commands are launched in their own process group. \fBjobs\fR
lists them, \fBfg\fR/\fBbg\fR resume them, \fBkill %N\fR signals a job and
\fBwait\fR reaps it. Ctrl-Z stops the foreground group; Ctrl-C interrupts it.
.SH LIMITATIONS
This is not a Bash replacement. Unsupported syntax is rejected with status 2
and a precise message; sharky never silently invokes Bash for a script.
Signal traps are stored but only the EXIT trap runs in v1.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR ruby (1)
